<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mykhailo Hrushevsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mykhailo Hrushevsky was a Ukrainian historian, author of a multi-volume History of Ukraine-Rus', and a leader of the national movement. In 1917 he chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-rada">Central Rada</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, which proclaimed the Ukrainian People's Republic and in January 1918 its independence. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> fell he went into exile, returned to Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 1924 and died under surveillance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mykhailo-hrushevsky.jpg" alt="Mykhailo Hrushevsky" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hrushevskyi_Mykhailo_XX.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Mykhailo Hrushevsky was born on 29 September 1866 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelm">Chełm</a>, then in Congress Poland, within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, to a Ukrainian noble family. His mother taught the children Ukrainian. He studied history at Kyiv University under Volodymyr Antonovych, and in 1894 was appointed to the new chair of Ukrainian history at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>. In Austrian Galicia he became president of the Shevchenko Scientific Society in 1897 and turned it into an institution resembling an academy of sciences. In 1899 he co-founded the National Democratic Party, which looked towards Ukrainian independence.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Russian Revolution of 1905</a> he returned to Russian-ruled Ukraine and co-founded the Ukrainian Scientific Society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>. Soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began he was arrested, accused of Mazepinism and Austrophilia, and in 1915 exiled to Simbirsk, later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. On 17 March 1917 he was elected head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-rada">Central Rada</a> in Kyiv. He guided it from autonomy within a democratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to full independence, and its government drafted a constitution of the Ukrainian People's Republic, adopted on 29 April 1918. After the coup of Pavlo Skoropadskyi he went into hiding. In 1919 he emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Hrushevsky wrote the first detailed scholarly synthesis of Ukrainian history, the ten-volume History of Ukraine-Rus', which covers the period from prehistory to the 1660s. He also wrote a History of Ukrainian Literature and an Illustrated History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and his bibliography has over 2,000 titles. The work broke with the Russocentric view that saw direct continuity from Rus' to Muscovy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. He regarded the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> as the legitimate heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, stressed the role of the common people, and saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a> as largely national and social. Omeljan Pritsak criticised him for neglecting the political elite.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hrushevsky returned to Soviet Ukraine in 1924, was elected to the All-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-academy-of-sciences-of-ukraine">Ukrainian Academy of Sciences</a> and went on writing his History. In 1931, after a long press campaign against him, he was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and soon arrested on a charge of belonging to a Ukrainian nationalist centre. He died on 24 November 1934 at Kislovodsk, of sepsis after minor surgery, and was buried at Baikove Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>. The speed with which an obituary was approved led to suspicion about his death.</p><p>He is regarded as one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>'s most prominent scholars and statesmen of the 20th century. Museums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> are devoted to him, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-academy-of-sciences-of-ukraine">Ukrainian Academy of Sciences</a> has published his Collected Works.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mykhailo Hrushevsky” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After their defeat in 1036 the weakened Pechenegs were replaced on the Pontic steppe by the Cumans and the Torks; <b>Mykhailo Hrushevsky</b> wrote that the horde moved to the Danube, crossed it and disappeared from the Pontic steppes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 1917 Ukrainian parties formed the Central Rada in Kyiv, chaired by the historian <b>Mykhailo Hrushevsky</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ukrainian-peoples-republic">Ukrainian People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelm">Chełm</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Kislovodsk, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.13,23.48&amp;year=1910" aria-label="Chełm on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mykhailo Hrushevsky"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4651 5738h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4651 5738h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.13,23.48&amp;year=1910">What was here in 1910? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
